<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Plantry.Composition.Infrastructure (plantry-g3da.9, ADR-024, ratified option B of plantry-swe3):
    the composition read layer's persistence home. Sits beside Plantry.Ai.Infrastructure as the second
    sanctioned context-free infrastructure project — it belongs to no bounded context, so it is
    deliberately absent from BoundaryTests' InfrastructureContexts sibling-reference registry (like
    Ai.Infrastructure and SharedKernel).

    Holds the one piece of state the dissolved Housekeeping bounded context ever owned: the Dismissal
    tombstone aggregate + HousekeepingDbContext (schema/table names unchanged — no data migration).
    Standing precedent for all future context dissolutions: layer-owned state (dismissals, view
    preferences, materialized roll-ups) lands here, never in Plantry.Web. Detector/read-model code
    that merely *reads* other contexts' schemas stays in Plantry.Web (ADR-021 cross-schema read models
    are not persistence).

    Deliberately has NO reference to Plantry.Web — Plantry.Web references THIS project instead. That is
    the whole point of the move: Plantry.Migrator (owner-credential container, ADR-016/017) and
    Plantry.Tests.Unit now depend on Plantry.Composition.Infrastructure for HousekeepingDbContext instead
    of on the deployable web app.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" Version="10.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Plantry.SharedKernel\Plantry.SharedKernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
